<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ventspils</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Windau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ventspils, German Windau, is a port city at the mouth of the Venta river in western Latvia. A Hanseatic town and later the main shipyard of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a>, it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1795 and grew after a railway linked it to the Russian interior in 1901. It remains one of the largest ice-free ports on the Baltic, especially for oil.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ventspils.jpg" alt="Ventspils" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edgars Šulcs · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ventspils_viduslaiku_pils_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
